\description{
An issuer may file the same document more than once: a DFP or ITR
re-filed to fix a figure, a scale, a note. \code{\link[=cvm_fetch]{cvm_fetch()}} returns the
current version of each document, as the CVM's yearly files do.
\code{cvm_versions()} returns every version the package knows of, the
current one included, so the figures of a filing can be followed
from one version to the next. The signature mirrors the part of
\code{\link[=cvm_fetch]{cvm_fetch()}} that identifies the documents (\code{dataset}, \code{table},
\code{cnpj}, \code{ticker}, \code{year}, \code{basis}) and takes none of its row
filters: \code{reading}, \code{from}/\code{to} and \code{as_of} select rows of the
current reading and are refused here.

Only the tables that keep a single filing per document (those whose
schema declares \code{keep_latest_version}: the DFP, ITR and FRE detail
tables, among others) have a version history. Asking for another
table, such as a dataset's \code{submissao} index or the CAD register,
aborts: those already return every row the CVM publishes. The list
of tables is in the section \emph{Arguments by table} of \code{\link[=cvm_fetch]{cvm_fetch()}}.
}
\section{Where the versions come from}{

The two backends know different parts of the history.
\itemize{
\item \code{source = "mirror"} (the default) reads, for every partition of
the table, the current asset \code{\link[=cvm_fetch]{cvm_fetch()}} reads plus the
\code{history} asset the ETL keeps next to it with the superseded
filings, dated. The history holds the filings the mirror saw being
replaced from 2026-10 on and, for the DFP and ITR statements, the
ones recovered from the CVM's filing system (RAD) back to 2010;
\code{history_source} says which (\code{"mirror"} or \code{"rad"}).
\item \code{source = "cvm"} reads the CVM's yearly CSV without collapsing it
to the current filing. That file keeps only the versions the CVM
still publishes, usually the current one alone, and carries no
supersession date, so \code{superseded_at}, \code{id_doc} and
\code{motivo_reapresentacao} are \code{NA} there; the call says so once. The
complete history is on the mirror.
}
}

\section{Columns}{

The columns of the table, \code{year} and \code{basis} as \code{\link[=cvm_fetch]{cvm_fetch()}}
returns them, then five version columns:
\itemize{
\item \code{superseded_at}: \code{Date} the version stopped being the current one
(the receipt date of the version that replaced it); \code{NA} for the
current version.
\item \code{superseded_by_versao}: the \code{versao} of the filing that replaced
it; \code{NA} for the current version.
\item \code{history_source}: \code{"current"} for the rows of the current version,
\code{"mirror"} or \code{"rad"} for a superseded version on the mirror,
\code{"cvm"} for a superseded version still in the CVM's yearly file.
\item \code{id_doc}: the CVM document id of the superseded filing, when the
history knows it.
\item \code{motivo_reapresentacao}: the reason the issuer gave for the
re-filing, as the CVM publishes it, when known.
}

Rows are ordered by issuer, reference date and \code{versao}. \code{versao} is
not a key on its own: the CVM index carries two filings under the
same \code{VERSAO} for some documents.
}

